---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const snapshot = [
  { label: 'Organization', value: 'Single-location dental practice, Veneto — over 30 years in business' },
  { label: 'Timeframe', value: 'Positioning in 2024–2025, CRM-driven campaign in 2026' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Positioning strategy design; end-to-end management of campaign, CRM and automations' },
  { label: 'Channels', value: 'Meta Ads (primary), Google Ads (legacy)' },
  { label: 'Tools', value: 'CRM with pipeline, WhatsApp automations, shared tracking sheets' },
];

const results = [
  { metric: '+600%', label: 'service line revenue in one year' },
  { metric: '46.5%', label: 'close rate (up from ~18-20% before)' },
  { metric: '~17.7×', label: '2026 campaign ROAS — mid-way figure' },
];
---
<CaseStudyLayout
  title="From a complex diagnosis to measurable revenue: positioning and automation in a single strategy"
  description="Case study: a proprietary clinical-communication protocol and a campaign with CRM and WhatsApp automation. Close rate more than doubled."
  eyebrow="Positioning · CRM · Automation"
  snapshot={snapshot}
  results={results}
>
  <h2>The context</h2>
  <p>
    A long-established practice, with a treatment line — periodontology — that's clinically solid
    but hard to communicate: the patient doesn't feel the urgency, the diagnosis is complex to
    explain, and competitors push solutions that are easier to sell. Alongside it, an implantology
    line with the opposite problem: clear demand, but leads easy to lose during follow-up.
  </p>

  <h2>The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Two complementary challenges: raising the credibility and conversion rate of the periodontal
    diagnosis, and building a system that follows every implantology lead through to the final
    decision, without losing it along the way.
  </p>

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    I designed the entire positioning strategy myself: a proprietary clinical-communication
    protocol that turns the diagnosis into a four-stage, understandable and objective journey —
    microbiological tests, laser technology, follow-up checks — communicated through dedicated
    materials. On the 2026 implantology campaign I managed the whole chain: campaign structure,
    CRM pipeline shared with the front desk, WhatsApp automations and nurturing flows for leads
    not yet ready.
  </p>

  <h2>Analysis and insight</h2>
  <p>
    The insight behind the protocol: the problem wasn't convincing the patient to get treated, but
    making them <em>see</em> the disease. Making the diagnosis objective with specific tests,
    clearly explained, shifts the conversation from price to health — and the numbers confirmed
    it, with a close rate more than doubling in a year.
  </p>
  <p class="open-point">
    A note on method: the growth of the periodontal line is the effect of the overall strategy —
    communication, materials, in-practice training, word of mouth — not of a single, isolatable
    campaign. The correlation with the protocol's introduction is strong, but the exact breakdown
    of factors isn't measurable with the data available. I'd rather say so.
  </p>

  <h2>Execution</h2>
  <p>
    For the positioning: a four-stage diagnostic journey, informational material compliant with
    healthcare communication regulations, in-practice training on how to explain the protocol. For
    the 2026 campaign: a CRM pipeline with explicit stages, meticulous tracking of every contact
    together with the front desk, WhatsApp automations built to work over time on leads stuck at
    the follow-up stage — the stage where, without automation, the most value is lost.
  </p>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p>
    The periodontal line went from around 30 diagnosed patients and 5-6 closed treatments (2024)
    to 114 diagnosed and 53 closed (2025): the close rate rose from ~18-20% to 46.5%, and revenue
    from that treatment alone from around €17,000 to over €120,000. The 2026 implantology
    campaign, at the midpoint of its run, had generated 33 leads and 4 patients for €31,680 in
    revenue on €1,785.78 in spend: a partial ROAS of around 17.7×, with 6 opportunities still
    being worked in the nurturing flows.
  </p>

  <h2>What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    That positioning and automation aren't separate activities: the first makes the patient
    receptive, the second stops that receptiveness from going to waste. And that treating the
    diagnosis as a moment of value — not as a bureaucratic step toward the quote — changes patient
    behavior more than any campaign optimization.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>
